course: religion-ethics
lesson: 33
title: Buddhism, humanism and respect for every belief
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: About 2500 years ago in India a prince called Siddhartha Gautama left his palace to find out why people suffer; Buddhists call him the Buddha, "the awakened one", and follow his path of wisdom and kindness.
explain: The Buddha taught the Four Noble Truths: life has suffering, suffering comes from craving, it can end, and the Eightfold Path leads there; many Buddhists meditate to calm the mind and do not believe in a creator God.
explain: Some people have no religion: an atheist believes there is no God, an agnostic thinks we cannot know, and humanists try to live good lives by reason and compassion, believing this life is the only one.
explain: Christians believe in God, yet freedom of religion means everyone may believe, practise, change or have no religion without being forced or mocked; respect does not mean agreeing, it means treating people fairly.
explain: Most faiths and humanism share honesty, kindness, care for the poor and the Golden Rule, so a Christian child can make friends across beliefs, learn real facts from people themselves and refuse stereotypes.
fact: Buddhism was founded by Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, in India about 2500 years ago.
fact: Buddhism aims to end suffering through wisdom and kindness.
fact: Many Buddhists do not believe in a creator God.
fact: The Four Noble Truths say: life has suffering, suffering comes from craving (always wanting more), suffering can end, and the Eightfold Path leads there.
fact: Meditation is quiet focusing of the mind; it is central in Buddhism and used by many other people too.
fact: Humanists believe this life is the only one and that people can be good using reason and compassion (caring about other people's pain), without God.
fact: An atheist believes there is no God; an agnostic thinks we cannot know whether there is.
fact: Freedom of religion is the right to believe, practise, change or have no religion; it is protected in human rights law.
fact: Respectful language means using the names people use for themselves and their faiths and not mocking holy things.
fact: Respecting someone's belief does not mean you have to agree with it.
fact: Most faiths and humanism value honesty, kindness, care for the poor and the Golden Rule.
fact: Interfaith friendship means people of different beliefs learning about each other and serving the community together.
fact: A stereotype judges a whole group by one picture; fair facts come from reliable sources and from members of the faith themselves.
exercise: Who founded Buddhism? | Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha | Guru Nanak, the first Guru | Muhammad, the last prophet | Abraham, the father of nations
exercise: What does the title "Buddha" mean? | The awakened one | The son of God | The last prophet | The holy king
exercise: What is the first of the Four Noble Truths? | Life has suffering | Life is always happy | God made the world | Every soul is equal
exercise: Where does suffering come from, according to the Buddha? | From craving, wanting too much | From the gods being angry | From bad luck at birth | From not going to the temple often enough
exercise: What did the Buddha teach leads to the end of suffering? | The Eightfold Path | The Five Pillars | The Ten Commandments | The five Ks
exercise: Why do many Buddhists meditate? | To calm and focus the mind | To ask a creator God for gifts | To keep their bodies from ageing | To win a place in the temple
exercise: What do many Buddhists believe about a creator God? | Many do not believe in one | They all believe in Brahman | They believe the Buddha is God | They believe in many creators
exercise: What do humanists believe? | People can be good by reason and compassion, without God | People can only be good if they pray each day | People should obey whatever the strongest say | People are born good and need no learning
exercise: What is an atheist? | A person who believes there is no God | A person who thinks we cannot ever know for sure | A person who prays to many different gods at home | A person who has lost their faith and wants it back
exercise: What is an agnostic? | A person who thinks we cannot know if God exists | A person who is sure there is no God | A person who worships nature, trees and the sun instead of God | A person who changes religion often
exercise: What does freedom of religion protect? | The right to believe, change or have no religion | Only the right to belong to the oldest church in the land | The right to make others join your religion | Only the right to pray at home in secret
exercise: Does respecting someone's belief mean agreeing with it? | No, it means treating them fairly anyway | Yes, you must agree or keep away | Yes, otherwise it is a kind of lying | No, it means pretending to have no faith
exercise: Which is an example of respectful language? | Calling people by the names they use for themselves | Using a funny joke name for their holy book to make friends laugh | Calling every faith except your own "a weird religion" | Saying only your own faith has truly good people in it
exercise: Which value do most faiths and humanism share? | The Golden Rule: treat others as you want to be treated | The rule that only priests may read | The rule that fasting is for everyone, even little babies | The rule that you must never eat meat
exercise: What is interfaith friendship? | People of different beliefs learning and serving together | People giving up their beliefs to agree | People trying to win arguments about God | People of one faith only meeting together
exercise: Where can you find fair facts about a faith? | From reliable books and from its own members | From jokes and rumours people tell in the playground | From the worst stories in the news | From people who dislike that faith
exercise: What is a stereotype? | Judging a whole group by one fixed picture | A careful study of a religion's holy books and history | A friendly visit to a place of worship with your class | A list of all the holy days in the year for each faith
exercise: Why did Siddhartha leave his palace? | To find out why people suffer | To become a king of another land | To build the first temple | To find a wife in the city
test: Mila doesn't believe in God and her dad is a humanist. Can she be a good person? | Yes, many people without religion live kind and honest lives | No, without God nobody can be good | Only if she secretly prays at night | Only when someone is watching her
test: lol Buddhists just sit doing nothing all day. Is that a fair description? | No, meditation trains the mind, and they also study and help others | Yes, Buddhism forbids any kind of work | Yes, that is the whole of the Eightfold Path | No, Buddhists never meditate at all
test: Tom says: "I'm not sure if God exists, nobody can really know." What is Tom? | An agnostic | An atheist | A humanist priest | A Buddhist monk
test: someone at school calls a classmate's faith "stupid nonsense." What is the best response? | Say it's not okay to mock beliefs; you can disagree politely | Laugh along so you are not left out | Mock that person's own faith back | Say nothing ever, because it is really none of your business at all
test: Your little brother: "If you respect Ali's faith, you've stopped being Christian!" Put him right. | Respect means treating people fairly; you keep your own faith | He is right, respect means joining their faith | He is right, Christians must avoid other faiths and their people | Respect means saying all faiths are the same
test: Kenji wants to change his religion when he grows up. What does freedom of religion say? | He has the right to change it | He must keep his parents' faith for ever | He must ask the government first | He may change only to the oldest faith
test: Grace keeps thinking "if I just had the new phone I'd be happy." Which Buddhist teaching speaks to this? | Suffering comes from craving | Life is always full of joy | Phones were made by the gods | The soul is reborn as a phone
test: Our church and the local mosque run a food bank together. What is this an example of? | Interfaith friendship and shared values | Christians giving up their own faith to join Islam | A mosque slowly turning itself into a church | A competition between them to win new members
test: olympiad judge: "Why should a Christian ask a Buddhist about Buddhism, not just read a meme?" | Members give real facts; memes often spread stereotypes | Memes are always better than people | Buddhists never tell anyone the truth about their own religion | Only priests should know about other faiths
test: Ella says atheists hate religious people. True? | No, an atheist believes there is no God; that is not hating anyone | Yes, all atheists hate believers | Yes, atheism means fighting churches | No, atheists believe in many gods
test: Which statement is fair to every belief? | People of all faiths and none deserve equal respect | Only people with a faith deserve respect | Only people with no faith at all can think clearly | Only the biggest religion in the country deserves respect
test: Which is the last of the Four Noble Truths? | The Eightfold Path leads to the end of suffering | Suffering will never end in this life | Craving makes people happy for ever | God will remove suffering at once
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the position designated as humanism? | That persons may live ethically through reason and compassion, without belief in God | That persons may live ethically solely through the observance of religious law | That persons ought to venerate humanity as a deity | That ethical conduct is impossible in the absence of faith
exam: Identify the principal cause of suffering as enunciated in the Four Noble Truths. | Craving or desire | The displeasure of the gods | Misfortune at birth | Neglect of temple worship
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning freedom of religion. | It encompasses the right to hold, practise, change or abandon a religion | It encompasses solely the right to practise the religion of one's parents | It permits the compulsion of others to adopt one's religion | It is confined to the freedom to worship in private
exam: Ascertain the distinction between an atheist and an agnostic. | The former holds there is no God; the latter that it cannot be known | The former holds that God exists; the latter that God does not | The former worships nature; the latter worships reason | There is no distinction whatsoever between the two terms
